| **1** | **Idempotency + PostgreSQL partial unique index** | `idempotency_key` is nullable, so the unique constraint is partial. A normal `ON CONFLICT` target could fail to infer the correct index. | `ON CONFLICT` repeats `WHERE idempotency_key IS NOT NULL`; same key + same body returns original result; same key + different body → `409`. | Test against **real PostgreSQL**, including concurrent identical requests and requests without an idempotency key. | ✅ Design resolved |
| **2** | **Resume could wake a delivery too early but not make it claimable** | Simply publishing a signal for a paused delivery would still fail the `next_retry_at <= now()` claim predicate. | Resume sets `next_retry_at = now()` for `ENDPOINT_PAUSED` deliveries, then publishes wake-up signals. | Verify resumed deliveries actually become claimable and are sent. Also verify repeated/old signals don't modify the retry schedule. | ✅ Design resolved |
| **3** | **Duplicate fan-out when endpoint has exact + `*` subscription** | An endpoint subscribed to both `user.created` and `*` could receive two deliveries for the same event. | Fan-out uses `EXISTS` / exact deduplication so one `(event, endpoint)` delivery is created. | Test endpoint with both subscriptions; expect exactly **one delivery**. | ✅ Design resolved |
| **4** | **Early RabbitMQ signals must not change retry timing** | Re-publishing an early `DeliverWebhook` signal must not push `next_retry_at` forward repeatedly. | Only creation of a new retry schedule changes `next_retry_at`; an early signal only re-publishes after the remaining delay. | Send multiple early/duplicate signals and verify `next_retry_at` stays unchanged. | ✅ Design resolved |
